        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "37. groupby Rules",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Groups CONSECUTIVE equal keys only.",
            "Sort by the same key first or you get fragments.",
            "Sub-iterators are shared and invalidated on advance.",
            "Wrap in `list()` if you need to keep a group."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "38. Combinatorics & tee"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`product`, `permutations`, `combinations`.",
            "Plus `combinations_with_replacement`.",
            "Sizes explode factorially — bound your inputs.",
            "`tee(it, n)` buffers up to the slowest consumer's gap."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "39. zip, Batches, Windows"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "zip(*[iter(x)] * 3)   # batching idiom",
            "batched(x, 3)         # 3.12+, readable",
            "pairwise(x)           # 3.10+ pairs",
            "accumulate(d, max, initial=0)  # fold"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`zip` is lazy in Py3 and stops at the shortest input.",
            "`zip_longest(fillvalue=…)` pads to the longest instead.",
            "`pairwise` gives overlapping pairs for deltas and windows.",
            "`accumulate` is a lazy running fold: sums, maxima, anything."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "40. Space Is The Headline"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "itertools primitives are C-implemented, not Python loops.",
            "They beat hand-rolled equivalents on speed and memory.",
            "Space: list O(n) vs generator O(1) — the reason to go lazy.",
            "Time: O(n) either way, but ~10-30% slower per tight loop."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Name It, Don't Build It",
          "text": "If your loop has an itertools name, the C version is already written, already tested, and already faster."
        },
        {
